7. DAILY TASK ENGINE AND LOCKING RULES
==================================================

This is a critical feature. Implement it as real business logic, not just visual labels.

Users create tasks for a specific challenge date.

A task may contain:
- Title
- Category: SAT, English, Fitness, or General
- Scheduled date
- Optional start time
- Optional reminder
- Optional description
- Optional estimated duration
- Optional priority
- Completion status
- Carry-forward preference
- Creation timestamp
- Completion timestamp
- Original task ID when carried forward

Task states:
- PENDING
- COMPLETED
- MISSED
- LOCKED
- ARCHIVED

Daily deadline:
- A task assigned to a challenge date can be completed on that date.
- By default, its completion window closes at 23:59:59 in Asia/Tashkent.
- At the next local calendar day, every incomplete task for the previous date becomes MISSED and LOCKED.
- Respect an optional custom deadline if the user explicitly sets one.
- Never allow a user to mark an expired task as newly completed through the normal interface.
- Show a clear explanation when a task is locked.
- Preserve all missed tasks in historical records.
- Do not delete task history to make the dashboard look better.

Carry-forward behavior:
- Incomplete tasks should move to the following day as new pending task instances.
- Preserve the original task as MISSED and LOCKED in its historical date.
- The new instance must display a subtle “O‘tgan kundan ko‘chirildi” label.
- Store the relationship between the original and carried task.
- Never create duplicate carry-forward instances for the same originating task and date.
- If a carried task is still incomplete, it may be carried forward again according to its carry-forward setting.
- Default carry-forward to ON for user-created tasks.
- Let the user disable carry-forward for tasks that must stay on a specific day.
- Completed tasks must never be carried forward.
- Allow the user to archive a carried-forward task.
- Do not silently replace a new scheduled task with an older carried-forward task.

Completion:
- A tick/check action changes a valid pending task to COMPLETED.
- Display the completion time.
- Update daily and overall progress immediately.
- Save the change persistently.
- Prevent double completion events and duplicate database records.

Deadline enforcement:
- Use consistent client-side validation for instant UI feedback.
- Validate completion attempts against the authoritative server time when connected.
- Implement appropriate database RPC or server-side validation for deadline-sensitive updates.
- Handle disconnected devices carefully without claiming that an unsynchronized offline completion is definitively server-verified.
- Never rely only on CSS or hidden buttons to enforce locking.

Daily progress must be calculated from actual tasks assigned to that date. Do not count missing plans as completed tasks.

